.SH DESCRIPTION
.B "WORK IN PROGRESS."
Drivel is unfinished and under-tested, and it
.B "will probably lose data"
for somebody before it stops being either. Do not use it for anything you
cannot afford to lose; keep backups, make them often, and check that you can
restore from them. The full statement \(em why, what has actually been run
against a real service, and the sharp edges that are known and not yet fixed
\(em is at
.UR https://syshlted.github.io/drivel/docs/status/
.UE ,
or in
.I docs/user/status.md
in the source tree.
.PP
.B drivel
mounts a local directory as a loopback FUSE filesystem that acts as an
.IR interceptor :
every operation is proxied to an underlying backing directory (the source of
truth / local cache) and, asynchronously and bidirectionally, synced with a
storage provider. Two providers ship:
.B "Google Drive"
\(em driven by the Drive API v3
.I changes.list
cursor feed, not webhooks and not the Workspace Events API \(em and
.BR SFTP ,
over an ordinary SSH account, with no software required on the server.
.PP
Filesystem operations never block on the network: reads, lookups, and attribute
calls pass straight through to the backing directory, while each mutation is
emitted as a change event and handled off the FUSE path by the sync engine.
Inbound changes from the provider are pulled into the backing directory by a
cursor-driven poll loop, with echo/loop suppression so a change that originated
locally is not re-applied when it echoes back from the remote. A provider with no
change feed of its own \(em SFTP has none \(em is polled instead by the
enumeration sweep, which makes
.I -sweep-interval
that mount's inbound latency rather than a long-period safety net.
.PP
.B drivel
is a client you run yourself. It ships no credentials of its own; you supply the
ones for the backend you point it at, so it runs under an identity you control.
Each provider uses its own transport: Drive traffic is HTTP/3 (QUIC) with an
automatic fallback to HTTP/2, and SFTP is the file transfer subsystem of SSH.
On a network that blocks UDP port 443, Drive traffic moves to HTTP/2 and sync
continues; when the block goes away, HTTP/3 is tried again within a few minutes.
A network that rejects the UDP packets switches over immediately. One that
silently discards them instead has to be waited out, so the request that runs
into it pauses \(em up to five seconds if the block was already there when
.B drivel
started, up to thirty if it appeared mid-transfer \(em and then completes over
HTTP/2. Nothing is lost and nothing above the transport sees an error. There is
nothing to configure either way.
.PP
A provider runs in a
.I separate process
that
.B drivel
starts for you. The backends that ship with
.B drivel
are carried in the binary itself, and it launches one by re-executing itself; a
backend from anywhere else is an executable named
.IB drivel-provider- kind
that
.B drivel
finds on a search path. Either way the process is separate so that a failure
inside a backend cannot take the filesystem down: a backend that crashes is
restarted underneath a mount that stays up, and pending transfers are retried.
See
.BR "PROVIDER BACKENDS" .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B mount-helper
The
.BR mount (8)
helper for
.IR /etc/fstab .
Normally reached through the
.I /sbin/mount.fuse.drivel
symlink rather than by this name: the helper is selected by
.BR argv [0],
so the same binary serves both. Linux only. See
.B MOUNT HELPER
below.
.TP
.B login
Interactive, rclone-style OAuth wizard. Collects the client ID/secret (from
flags, an existing
.IR credentials.json ,
or prompts), writes
.IR credentials.json ,
runs the loopback/paste login flow, and caches the resulting token to
.IR token.json .
With
.B \-account
it also adds an
\fB[account.\fINAME\fB]\fR
table and a matching
.B [[mount]]
to the config file, appending to it and never rewriting it: an entry that is
already there is printed for you to reconcile by hand rather than replaced.
This is the only interactive subcommand.
.TP
.B mount
Mount a directory and sync it. Non-interactive: it requires a token produced by
.B login
and never prompts on stdin. This is the default command, so
.B drivel -mount \fIdir\fB ...
(with a leading flag and no subcommand word) also selects it.
.TP
.B completion \fIshell\fR
Print a shell completion script on standard output, for
.B bash
or
.BR zsh .
The script is rendered from this binary's own flag definitions when the command
runs, so it always describes the flags this build has. Nothing is installed by
running it; the caller decides where the output goes.
.IP
To enable completion in the current shell:
.IP
.EX
eval "$(drivel completion bash)"
eval "$(drivel completion zsh)"
.EE
.IP
That runs
.B drivel
at every shell start. To install it once instead, write it where the shell looks
\(em for bash,
.I ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/drivel
or
.IR /usr/share/bash-completion/completions/drivel ;
for zsh, a file named
.I _drivel
in a directory on
.IR $fpath .
For zsh the file is the better route rather than merely the tidier one, and the
.B eval
form must come after
.B compinit
in
.IR ~/.zshrc ,
because
.B compinit
is what defines the
.B compdef
the script uses to register itself. Placed earlier it reports that and does
nothing.
.TP
.B status \fR[\fIpath\fR ...]
Ask a running
.B drivel mount -control
what it is doing, over its control socket. With no
.IR path ,
one line per mount: its state
.RB ( idle ,
.BR syncing ,
.BR paused ,
.B saturated
while writes through the mount wait on uploads,
.BR sweeping )
and whether its backend is
.BR online ,
.B offline
or
.BR unknown .
Reachability is observed from drivel's own requests, including a lazy mount's
downloads of files it is asked to read, and never probed; with no traffic for an
hour it is
.BR unknown .
.IP
With a
.IR path ,
inside one of the mounts: a file's sync status, or, for a directory, one line
per entry in it
.RB ( -d
reports the directory itself instead, and
.B -limit
caps the listing, default 1000). The statuses are
.BR synced ,
.BR pending ,
.BR uploading ,
.BR retrying ,
.B error
(an upload given up on, which the next sweep re-sends, or a remote change that
failed to apply here, which is retried),
.BR placeholder ,
.B conflict
(a conflict copy of it was kept),
.B local-only
(a conflict copy kept on this machine, a special file, or a drivel temporary:
deliberately not synced), and
.BR unknown ,
which means no record vouches for the file and is
.B not
a synonym for synced. A directory's status is never a roll-up of its contents.
Nothing here contacts the backend.
.B -json
prints the API's responses as they are.
.TP
.B pause
Hold a running mount's uploads, change-feed polling and sweep, for
.B -for
(default
.BR 1h ,
at most
.BR 24h ).
Writes through the mount are still accepted and never block; they go out when the
pause ends. An upload already running finishes, and in lazy mode a placeholder
that is opened is still fetched. A pause ends on its own when it expires, when it
is holding more than ten thousand changes (with a warning), or when the mount
stops, and is never kept across a restart. Every mount of the process, or the one
named by
.BR -name .
.TP
.B resume
End a pause early.
.TP
.B stop
Shut a running drivel down exactly as
.B SIGTERM
would \(em unmount, drain queued uploads, exit \(em and wait until it has.
.IP
All four take
.BI -socket " path"
for a mount started with
.BR -control-socket ,
and need the mount to have been started with
.B -control
(or an fstab line with
.BR control ).
With
.BI -name " name"
and no
.BR -socket ,
.BR status ,
.B pause
and
.B resume
first look for that mount's own socket, where an fstab line puts it:
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/drivel/name.sock
or
.IR /run/drivel/uid/name.sock .
The protocol is HTTP and JSON over the socket, documented for other tools in
.I docs/dev/control-api.md
in the source tree.
.TP
.B help
Print top-level usage and exit. The flag spellings
.BR -h ,
.B -help
and
.B --help
do the same when they are the first argument; after a subcommand they belong to
that subcommand, so
.B "drivel mount -h"
lists the mount options.
.SH MOUNT OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -config " file"
TOML file describing one or more mounts; see
.BR CONFIGURATION .
When neither
.B -config
nor
.B -mount
is given,
.B drivel
reads
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/drivel/config.toml
if it exists, so
.B "drivel mount"
on its own serves everything configured.
.IP
It cannot be combined with any flag that describes
.I what
to mount \(em that would require a precedence rule between the two \(em though
.BR -debug ,
.BR -pprof ,
.B -name
and
.B -account
compose with it.
.TP
.BI -name " name"
Serve only the mount of this name, instead of every mount in the config file.
The name is the one a mount reports in its log lines: the
.B name
key, or the account name, or the mountpoint's basename. Names are unique, so this
selects exactly one mount.
.IP
It
.I selects
among the mounts a config file describes and never describes one itself, which is
why it composes with
.B -config
where
.B -mount
and the rest cannot \(em and why it requires a config file. Given alongside
.B -mount
it is an error rather than a flag that is quietly disregarded. A name matching no
mount is a startup error listing the names the file does define.
.TP
.BI -account " name"
Serve only the mounts belonging to this account \(em the ones whose
.B account
key is
.IR name .
An account may own several mounts, so unlike
.B -name
this may select more than one. It cannot be combined with
.BR -name ,
which already identifies a single mount, and it is otherwise the same kind of
flag: a selector, requiring a config file to select from.
.IP
Note that
.B -account
means something different on each of the three paths that has one. Here it
selects from a config file;
.B "drivel login -account"
creates an account; and
.B account=
in an fstab line names where the credentials for that one line come from. An
fstab line selects from a config file with
.B name=
instead, since one line is one mount.
.TP
.B -list
Print the mounts the config file describes and exit, having mounted none of them.
One row per mount, with its name, its account, the provider it will talk to and
its mountpoint. The provider column is the one a config file cannot show you
directly: the kind is written once in an
.B [account.*]
table that each mount then refers to by key, so a file holding both a Drive mount
and an SFTP mount never puts the two side by side.
.IP
Every row is
.I resolved
rather than echoed back \(em relative paths expanded, defaults filled in, an
account's provider settings merged with the mount's, and the name the derived one
where there is no
.B name
key \(em because it is the same resolution mounting performs, so the listing
describes the mount you would actually get. Note what that does not make it: it
fails on a mount it cannot resolve at all, but it does
.B not
apply the guards that refuse two mounts sharing a mountpoint or a state DB. A
listing is what you reach for when a config file is misbehaving, and one that
refused to print until the file was already correct would be no use then.
.IP
It reads a config file and mounts nothing, which decides what it combines with.
Like
.B -name
and
.BR -account ,
with which it composes, it needs a config file and refuses every flag that
describes a mount. Unlike them it also refuses
.BR -debug ,
.B -pprof
and
.BR -pprof-allow-remote ,
which would have nothing to act on in a command that exits without serving
anything.
.IP
The table goes to standard output, so it pipes; the file it was read from goes to
standard error, since without
.B -config
that file was discovered rather than named.
.TP
.B -full
With
.BR -list ,
widen each row with five more columns:
.BR DATA ,
the backing directory, or
.B "(in place)"
for an in-place mount;
.BR STATE ,
the sync-state DB, which is derived from the mount's name and so appears nowhere
in the config file unless
.B state
was set by hand;
.BR MODE ,
one of
.BR lazy ,
.B eager
or
.BR log-only ;
.BR SWEEP ,
the re-enumeration interval, which on SFTP is the poll interval; and
.BR MAX-DELETES ,
the cap on deletions one reconcile may infer. The last two read
.B -
for a log-only mount, which never enumerates and so applies neither. On its own,
without
.BR -list ,
it is an error rather than a flag that does nothing.
.TP
.BI -mount " dir"
Path to mount the filesystem. Required unless
.B -config
is used. Created if missing.
.TP
.BI -data " dir"
Backing directory used as the source of truth. If omitted,
.B drivel
uses
.I in-place mode
(Linux only): the mountpoint is its own backing store, so files remain in it
after
.B drivel
exits. See
.BR "IN-PLACE MODE" .
.TP
.BI -credentials " file"
OAuth client-secret JSON. Supplying it enables Drive sync; without it,
.B drivel
runs in log-only mode (no network, no auth) and merely reports the change events
it would sync.
.TP
.BI -token " file"
Path to the cached OAuth token written by
.BR "drivel login" .
Default:
.IR token.json .
.TP
.BI -state " file"
Path to the sync-state database (the change-feed cursor and echo-suppression
records). Kept outside the backing tree so it is not itself synced. Default:
.IR drivel-state.db .
.TP
.BI -index " file"
Path to the provider's path\(->ID index: a cache that lets a restart resolve
remote objects without re-deriving every mapping from the API. It is never
authoritative \(em entries are verified against the remote before they are used,
and anything missing is looked up again \(em so deleting this file costs API
round trips and nothing else. Kept outside the backing tree. An empty value
disables it. Default:
.IR drivel-index.db .
.TP
.BI -drive-root " id"
Drive folder ID mapped to the mount root. Use
.B root
for the My Drive root. Default:
.BR root .
.IP
An
.IR id ,
not a folder path and not a folder name: the last segment of the folder's address
in the Drive web interface,
.IR drive.google.com/drive/folders/ID .
Drivel verifies it the first time it is used and refuses the mount if it names
nothing this account can see, a file rather than a folder, or a folder in the
Drive trash \(em because a root it cannot list is indistinguishable from a Drive
whose every file was deleted.
.TP
.BI -drive-sweep-mode " mode"
How the enumeration sweep walks Drive.
.B scoped
descends from
.B -drive-root
one directory at a time, so the cost is proportional to the folder mounted.
.B flat
lists the whole account, one request per thousand objects, and filters afterwards.
.B auto
(the default) descends unless
.B -drive-root
names the whole Drive, where there is no subtree to save.
.IP
A descent spends roughly one request per directory, where a flat listing spends one
per thousand objects in the account, so a subtree holding a very large number of
near-empty directories can be cheaper to sweep
.BR flat .
Drivel logs when a descent looks folder-dense rather than leaving it to be
discovered.
.TP
.BI -drive-delete " mode"
What removing a file does to the remote copy.
.B trash
(the default) moves it to the Drive trash, where it can be restored for 30 days.
.B permanent
unlinks it outright, with no undo anywhere, which is what Drivel did before this
option existed.
.IP
The default is the recoverable one because not every deletion Drivel performs was
typed by a user: an enumeration sweep infers deletions from a baseline, and a
wrong premise makes the inference wrong with it. Choose
.B permanent
when the mount is how storage gets reclaimed \(em a trashed file still counts
against the account's quota until the trash is emptied. An unknown value is
refused at startup.
.TP
.BI -drive-overwrite-check " mode"
Which uploads check, once they finish, for an edit made on Drive while they ran.
Drive cannot refuse an upload over a changed file, so a checked upload compares
the file's revision history from before it with after, and keeps any edit that
arrived in between as a conflict copy, at the cost of two extra requests.
.B large
(the default) checks uploads of 4 MiB or more;
.B always
checks every upload of a file the mount has synced before;
.B off
checks none, leaving an overwritten edit only in the file's Drive revision
history. An unknown value is refused at startup.
.TP
.B -lazy
Enable lazy hydration. Remote files are materialised in the backing directory as
.IR placeholders :
they carry the real name, size and modification time but occupy no space, and
their content is fetched the first time something reads (or partially writes)
them. A directory listing therefore costs no downloads. Requires
.BR -credentials ,
since a placeholder is a promise that the content can be fetched later.
.IP
Without this flag the backing directory holds full content, which is the default.
.IP
Placeholder state is recorded in an extended attribute on each backing file
.RB ( user.drivel.placeholder
on Linux and macOS;
.B drivel.placeholder
in
.B EXTATTR_NAMESPACE_USER
on FreeBSD), which is what stops the sync engine from uploading a placeholder's zero
bytes over the real remote file. It is the only such record: if the backing
filesystem cannot store user extended attributes,
.B drivel
refuses to start a lazy mount there, because an unmarked placeholder is
indistinguishable from an empty file. Use eager mode or a different
.I -data
directory; no database stands in for the attribute.
.TP
.B -xattr
Serve extended attributes through the mountpoint by passing them to the backing
store. Off by default: without it,
.B drivel
answers every xattr operation on the mount with
.BR EOPNOTSUPP ,
as a filesystem with no xattr support does.
.IP
The default is a safety property, not a performance one. A
.B drivel
mount is an interceptor rather than a plain loopback, and its own control metadata
lives in a
.B user.drivel.placeholder
attribute on the backing file (see
.BR -lazy ).
Passing attributes through publishes that marker at the mountpoint and makes it
writable by anything that can write there: stripping it from an unhydrated
placeholder makes the next upload replace the remote file with the placeholder's
zero bytes, and attaching it to an ordinary file makes the next read fetch the
remote copy over local content. Neither requires privilege and neither is
reported.
.IP
Nothing inside
.B drivel
needs the passthrough \(em the hydrator reads and writes its marker on the backing
file directly, below the mount \(em so enabling it is for applications on the
mountpoint that want extended attributes of their own. Extended attributes are
not synced to the provider in any case, whatever this is set to.
.TP
.B -resync
Enumerate the whole remote tree and reconcile it against the backing directory at
startup, even when a baseline already exists.
.IP
A sweep runs automatically without this flag in three cases: the first run (there
is no change cursor yet, so the remote tree has never been looked at), a sweep that
a previous run left unfinished, and a change cursor the provider says has expired.
It lists metadata only \(em roughly one request per 1000 objects, no content \(em
and runs in the background, so the mount comes up immediately and stays usable
while it proceeds.
.IP
The change-feed start token is taken
.I before
the sweep and adopted only after it finishes, so nothing that changes during the
sweep is missed.
.TP
.B -materialize
During a reconcile, create local copies of remote files that have none. Under
.B -lazy
this always happens and costs nothing (the copy is a placeholder), so the flag is
only meaningful in the default eager mode, where materialising means downloading
the entire remote tree \(em a decision the user makes rather than a side effect of
mounting.
.TP
.BI -max-deletes " n"
Cap on the deletions one reconcile may infer, in either direction. Default:
.BR 100 ;
.B 0
removes the limit.
.IP
Exceeding the cap abandons the delete pass entirely rather than trimming it. A
reconcile only ever infers a deletion for a path it has previously synced (never
from a file merely being absent on one side), so a large count almost always means
the premise is wrong rather than that there are that many real deletions \(em a
state database reused against a different
.BR -drive-root ,
a fresh empty backing directory, a mount pointing somewhere new. A deletion the cap
does not catch is still recoverable: under the default
.B -drive-delete trash
the remote object is moved to the Drive trash rather than destroyed, and Drive keeps
it for 30 days. That is the guard behind this one rather than a reason to relax it,
and
.B -drive-delete permanent
removes it.
.IP
A refused pass still counts as a completed sweep, so raising the cap and restarting
does not retry it. If the deletions are genuine, re-run with
.B -resync
as well \(em otherwise nothing happens until the next
.B -sweep-interval
falls due.
.TP
.BI -sweep-interval " d"
Re-enumerate and reconcile the remote tree this often, timed from the last
.I completed
sweep rather than from process start. Default:
.BR 24h ;
.B 0
disables it.
.IP
The change feed only reports what happens while
.B drivel
is watching, so anything that happened while it was not running stays
unreconciled until something enumerates the tree again. Without a periodic sweep
that means a first run, a resumed sweep, an expired cursor or
.BR -resync ,
which for a long-lived mount can be never.
.IP
.B 0
disables the
.I schedule
and not the repair sweep: an upload that was abandoned still brings an
enumeration forward a few minutes later, because
.B 0
means \(lqdo not poll\(rq rather than \(lqnever repair\(rq. See
.B ABANDONED UPLOADS
below.
.TP
.BI -push-delay " d"
How long a file must go unchanged before it is uploaded. Default:
.BR 300ms .
Must be greater than zero; an explicit
.B 0
is refused rather than read as \(lquse the default\(rq.
.IP
The window coalesces: a file saved three times inside it is uploaded once. It is
short by default because the events it merges are already coarse \(em a write is
noticed when the file is
.IR closed ,
not on every
.BR write (2),
so copying a large file produces two events rather than millions. Raising it
trades promptness for quota, and drains the outbound queue on a longer cycle,
which is what keeps a bulk copy of many small files from filling that queue and
slowing the filesystem itself to the speed of the uplink. When that queue does
fill,
.B drivel
logs one
.B back-pressure
line on entering the condition and one on leaving it, naming the depth and this
option \(em it is waiting on purpose, and dropping the events instead would lose
files.
.IP
It cannot hold a file indefinitely: one changed again inside every window is still
uploaded, on a bound derived from the delay. It has no effect at all on a file
that is held open and never closed \(em a database, a VM image, a log, a new file
still being written \(em whose
changes are not seen until it is closed and are otherwise picked up by
.BR -sweep-interval .
And it does not reduce conflicts; a longer window leaves more time for the remote
to change underneath, so it makes a conflict copy marginally more likely.
.TP
.BI -upload-workers " n"
How many files this mount uploads at once. Default:
.BR 4 .
Must be 1 or more; an explicit
.B 0
is refused rather than read as \(lquse the default\(rq.
.IP
Writes to the same path are serialised whatever this is set to, because concurrent
uploads of one file can land out of order; the concurrency is across files. Raising
it buys less than it appears to: every push shares one HTTP/3 connection and so one
congestion window, meaning extra workers overlap the per-file round trips rather
than moving more bytes. Each in-flight upload can also hold a chunk buffer \(em
16 MiB on Drive \(em and past the point the provider begins throttling, more
workers cost quota and gain nothing.
.TP
.BI -hydrate-workers " n"
How many placeholders this mount fetches at once under
.BR -lazy .
Default:
.BR 8 .
Must be 1 or more, on the same grounds as
.BR -upload-workers .
Ignored in eager mode, where nothing hydrates.
.IP
This is what bounds a recursive read over a lazy tree, which would otherwise fault
every file simultaneously. It is higher than the upload default because a typical
link's downlink is several times its uplink, and because it sits on the read path:
a caller is blocked on every fetch it governs, so
.B 1
turns a parallel read into a queue.
.TP
.BI -conflicts " action"
Where the losing side of a conflict goes:
.B dir
(the default) keeps it in a
.B .drivel-conflicts
folder in the same directory;
.B local-trash
puts it in the desktop trash under
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash ;
.B remote-trash
puts it in the backend's trash, and is refused on a backend without one, such as
SFTP;
.B overwrite
keeps nothing, and also skips the check a first upload makes for a remote file at
the same path. See
.BR CONFLICTS .
.TP
.BI -conflicts-dir " place"
With
.BR "-conflicts dir" ,
where the
.B .drivel-conflicts
folders live:
.B both
(the default) syncs them like any other folder,
.B local
keeps them on the machine where the conflict happened, and
.B remote
keeps them on the remote only. Refused beside any other
.BR -conflicts .
.IP
Both counts are per mount. A limit shared between mounts would let either one
starve the other and let each infer, from how long it waited, when the other was
busy; Drivel budgets nothing across mounts. Neither number governs the inbound
change feed, which applies remote changes one at a time and has no knob.
.TP
.B -debug
Enable FUSE debug logging.
.TP
.BI -pprof " addr"
Serve Go's
.B net/http/pprof
profiling endpoints on
.I addr
(for example
.BR localhost:6060 ,
or a bare
.B 6060
for the same thing), giving
.B go tool pprof
goroutine, heap, CPU and trace profiles of the running process. One endpoint
serves the whole process, not one per mount. Empty, the default, starts nothing.
.IP
This endpoint serves the process's heap \(em which for a filesystem holds the
paths, and in a buffer somewhere the contents, of the files being synced \(em to
anyone who can reach it, and lets them start a CPU profile. It therefore binds
only a loopback address: anything else is refused at startup unless
.B -pprof-allow-remote
is also given. Bind it to localhost and tunnel to it. A port that cannot be
bound is a startup error rather than a warning, so a run started in order to be
measured never silently produces nothing.
.IP
.B /debug/pprof/cmdline
is not served. It would return this process's command line, which names the
credentials file, the token, the backing directory and the account.
.TP
.B -pprof-allow-remote
Permit
.B -pprof
to bind an address that is not loopback, publishing the profiling endpoint \(em
and so the paths and some contents of synced files \(em to whoever can reach
that address. Refused on its own, without
.BR -pprof .
.TP
.B -control
Serve the control & status API on a unix socket, so that
.BR "drivel status" ,
.BR pause ,
.B resume
and
.B stop
\(em and other tools \(em can reach this process. One socket serves every mount
the process runs. Off by default: the socket names every synced path and can stop
the process. It lives in a directory that must be owned by this user and mode
.BR 0700 ,
is itself
.BR 0600 ,
and a connection from another uid is refused where the platform reports one. A
socket left by a crashed drivel is replaced; one a live drivel is answering on
is not, and the second process refuses to start. An fstab line asks for it with
the
.B control
helper option instead, which gives each line a socket of its own; see
.BR "MOUNT HELPER" .
.TP
.BI -control-socket " path"
Where
.B -control
puts its socket, instead of
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/drivel/control.sock .
With
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
unset there is no default, and
.B -control
alone is refused. Refused without
.BR -control .
.SH LOGIN OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -account " name"
Store this login as a named account: its credentials and token go in
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/drivel/name ,
created mode 0700, and
.BI [account. name ]
is appended to the config file.
.IP
Without it,
.B drivel login
behaves as it always has and writes to the current directory. With several
accounts a shared token file is not a limitation but a hazard: each login would
silently overwrite the previous one's credentials.
.TP
.BI -config " file"
Config file to add the account to. Default:
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/drivel/config.toml .
Only ever appended to \(em an account that is already defined is printed for you
to reconcile by hand rather than rewritten, so comments and layout survive.
.TP
.BI -credentials " file"
Path to read/write the OAuth client-secret JSON. Default:
.IR credentials.json .
.TP
.BI -token " file"
Path to write the OAuth token. Default:
.IR token.json .
.TP
.BI -client-id " id"
OAuth client ID. If omitted, it is read from
.I -credentials
or prompted for.
.TP
.BI -client-secret " secret"
OAuth client secret. If omitted, it is read from
.I -credentials
or prompted for.
.TP
.BI -project-id " id"
GCP project ID (optional).
.TP
.BI -scope " name"
Drive scope: one of
.BR drive ", " drive.readonly ", or " drive.file .
If omitted, the wizard shows a menu.
.TP
.BI -port " n"
Loopback port for the OAuth redirect;
.B 0
picks a free port automatically. Default:
.B 53682
(rclone's port).
.TP
.B -open
Attempt to open the authorization URL with the operating system's browser
handler.
.SH MOUNT HELPER
Installed as
.I /sbin/mount.fuse.drivel
(and
.IR /sbin/mount.drivel ),
drivel is a
.BR mount (8)
helper, so a mount can be described in
.I /etc/fstab
and brought up by
.BR mount ,
.B mount -a
or systemd at boot with no terminal attached.
.B Linux only.
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
work  /home/j/Drive  fuse.drivel  _netdev,nofail,run\-as=j,account=work,data=/var/cache/drivel/work  0 0
.EE
.in
.PP
Prefer the
.B fuse.drivel
type: go\-fuse mounts as
.RB \(dq fuse. \(dq
plus its subtype, so that is what
.I /proc/self/mountinfo
reports whichever type is written, and systemd's fstab\-generator compares the
two.
.PP
The device field (column 1) names the mount: with
.B config=
it selects the
.B [[mount]]
entry, otherwise it becomes the mount's name and the source
.BR findmnt (8)
shows.
.B drivel
and
.B none
are treated as filler and the mountpoint's basename is used instead.
.SS Helper options
Given with
.BR -o .
Either
.B config=
or the shaping options, never both \(em the same rule
.B -config
enforces on the command line.
.TP
.BI config= file
Serve the
.B [[mount]]
entry of
.I file
that this line names. Its
.B path
must equal the fstab mountpoint.
.TP
.BI account= name
Credentials and token from
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/drivel/name ,
as
.B drivel login -account
.I name
wrote them.
.TP
.BR data= ", " credentials= ", " token= ", " state= ", " index= ", " name=
As the corresponding
.B mount
flags. Paths must be absolute, or begin with
.RB \(dq ~/ \(dq
for the mounting account's home.
.TP
.BR drive-root= ", " drive-delete= ", " drive-sweep-mode= ", " drive-overwrite-check=
As the corresponding
.B mount
flags. Note
.BR drive-delete= ,
which decides whether a removal is recoverable: a boot mount is the one nobody is
watching, and
.B drive-delete=permanent
is how such a mount reclaims space rather than filling the trash.
.TP
.BR lazy ", " xattr ", " resync ", " materialize ", " "max-deletes=" ", " "sweep-interval=" ", " "push-delay=" ", " "upload-workers=" ", " "hydrate-workers=" ", " "conflicts=" ", " "conflicts-dir="
As the corresponding
.B mount
flags.
.TP
.BI run-as= name
Run the daemon as
.IR name :
move
.BR HOME " and the " XDG_ *
variables to that account and give up root before reading any credential. This
is what makes a boot mount own its files as the user rather than as root. Not
spelled
.BR user= ,
which is fstab's own \(lqwho may mount this\(rq and is passed down by
.BR mount (8)
to record who did.
.TP
.BI logfile= path
Where the daemon logs. Without it the daemon's output goes to
.IR /dev/null ,
because a backgrounded process holding the caller's stdout open would block
anything reading it through a pipe. Startup failures still reach
.BR mount (8)
either way.
.TP
.B foreground
Do not fork. For debugging; an fstab line must not use it.
.TP
.B control
Serve the control socket for this line's mount, as
.B -control
does. Each fstab line is its own process, so the socket is named after the
mount:
.I /run/drivel/uid/name.sock
when the helper starts as root, where
.I uid
is the
.B run-as=
account's and the directory is made by root and handed to that account before
root is given up;
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/drivel/name.sock
otherwise. A second line with the same name refuses to mount.
.TP
.BI control-socket= path
With
.BR control ,
the socket's path instead.
.TP
.BI mount-timeout= d
Give up if the filesystem is not live within
.IR d ,
killing the child. Off by default: systemd already bounds a mount unit's
startup.
.TP
.BR debug ", " fsname= ", " allow_other ", " allow_root ", " default_permissions ", " nosuid ", " nodev ", " noexec ", " noatime ", " sync ", " dirsync ", " auto_unmount
Tracing, the source name, and mount options passed to the backend verbatim.
.PP
.BR defaults ", " auto ", " noauto ", " _netdev ", " nofail ", " user ", " users ", " nouser ", " owner ", " group ", " rw ,
the
.B atime
family,
.B comment=
and every
.BR x- *
option belong to
.BR mount (8)
or systemd and are accepted and ignored.
.BR ro ", " remount ", " bind " and " move
are
.IR refused :
drivel has no read\-only mode, and a line claiming one over a writable mount
would be a lie. An unknown option is an error unless
.B -s
(sloppy) is given.
.SS Checking a line
.B mount -f
validates an fstab line \(em options, the
.B run-as
account, the resolved mount \(em without mounting anything or dropping
privileges.
.B mount -v
logs what it resolved. To test against a file other than
.IR /etc/fstab ,
use
.BR "mount -a -T ./my-fstab" ;
.BR umount (8)
has no such option, so unmount by path.
.PP
See
.I docs/user/fstab.md
for the full guide.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.PP
The config file describes any number of mounts, which one
.B drivel
process serves together. A single mount needs nothing beyond its flags; the file
is what you graduate to when one stops being enough.
.PP
An
.I account
is a set of credentials \(em a provider kind plus that provider's settings. A
.I mount
references an account and says what to mount from it. Their provider settings are
merged, with the mount's winning, so one account can be mounted more than once
with different roots.
.PP
.RS
.EX
[account.personal]
provider    = "gdrive"
credentials = "/home/you/.config/drivel/personal/credentials.json"
token       = "/home/you/.config/drivel/personal/token.json"

[[mount]]
account = "personal"
path    = "~/drive-personal"
data    = "~/.local/share/drivel/mounts/personal"
lazy    = true

[mount.provider]
root = "root"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Mount keys mirror the flags:
.IR name ,
.IR account ,
.IR path ,
.IR data ,
.IR state ,
.IR lazy ,
.IR xattr ,
.IR debug ,
.IR resync ,
.IR materialize ,
.IR max-deletes ,
.IR sweep-interval ,
.IR push-delay ,
.IR upload-workers ,
.IR hydrate-workers ,
.I conflicts
and
.IR conflicts-dir .
An unrecognised key is an error rather than a silent no-op. Relative paths resolve
against the config file's own directory, so a config directory can be moved or
kept in a dotfiles repository. Within a
.B [mount.provider]
or account table, a value is treated as a path only when written like one \(em
beginning
.RI \(dq ~/ \(dq,
.RI \(dq ./ \(dq
or
.RI \(dq ../ \(dq
\(em since a folder ID is a string too.
.SS Provider accounts
An account's remaining keys belong to its provider and are never interpreted by
the config layer.
.B gdrive
takes
.IR credentials ,
.IR token ,
.IR root ,
.IR index ,
.IR sweep-mode ,
.I delete
and
.IR scope ,
described under MOUNT OPTIONS above.
.PP
.B sftp
takes
.I host
and optionally
.IR port " (22),"
.I user
(required),
.I key
and
.I certificate
for public-key authentication,
.IR agent " (true \(em uses"
.BR $SSH_AUTH_SOCK ),
.IR known-hosts " (" ~/.ssh/known_hosts ),
.I root
(the remote directory the mount maps to; empty means the login directory),
.IR connect-timeout " (30s)"
and
.IR concurrent-requests .
There is no password or passphrase option and there will not be one; load an
encrypted key into an agent instead.
.PP
.RS
.EX
[account.server]
provider = "sftp"
host     = "files.example.com"
user     = "you"
key      = "~/.ssh/id_ed25519"
root     = "/srv/share"

[[mount]]
account        = "server"
path           = "~/share"
data           = "~/.local/share/drivel/mounts/server"
sweep\-interval = "5m"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Two things differ from Drive and matter. The server's host key must already be in
.I known_hosts
\(em drivel never trusts a key on first use and offers no option to, because an
.BR fstab (5)
mount at boot has nobody to answer a prompt. And SFTP has no change feed, so
.I -sweep-interval
is the poll interval for inbound changes and its 24h default is wrong; see
.I docs/user/sftp.md
for both.
.PP
Mounts are validated against each other before any of them is opened, because
several mounts can damage each other in ways one cannot. Two mounts may not share
a sync-state database (each engine would read the other's echo records as its own
baseline, which is also what reconcile infers deletions from), a mountpoint, or a
name; a backing directory may not sit inside another mount's mountpoint, or its
reads would be routed back through
.BR drivel ;
and a state database may not sit inside any backing tree, or it would sync itself
to the cloud and its own writes would generate the events causing more.
.PP
Separate
.B drivel
processes cannot see each other's configuration, so each mount also writes a
marker,
.IR .drivel-backing ,
at its backing directory's root and locks it while running. A mount refuses to
start when its backing directory, mountpoint or state database lies inside the
backing directory of another running
.BR drivel ,
or when another one already serves the same directory; a marker whose mount is
not running is only noted. Any subdirectory holding a marker, and on Linux any
other
.B drivel
mountpoint, is left to that mount by every part of this one: nothing in it is
uploaded, even when written through this mount, nothing is downloaded into it,
and a remote deletion does not reach inside it. A rename into one is a deletion
here, and a rename out of one a new file. The marker itself is never synced in
either direction, and a file another program holds an exclusive
.BR flock (2)
on \(em another
.BR drivel 's
state database, for one \(em is not uploaded while the lock is held. That
includes a lock taken through the mount, on Linux, where such locks are passed
to the backing file; a write made under one is uploaded once it is released.
.BR fcntl (2)
locks are not passed through.
.SH IN-PLACE MODE
Omitting
.I -data
selects in-place mode, in which the mount directory is its own backing store.
.B drivel
opens a directory handle to the mountpoint
.I before
mounting over it and routes all backing I/O through
.RI ( /proc/self/fd/N )
so it bypasses the FUSE overlay instead of recursing into it. Nothing is copied,
and the files remain in place after unmount. In-place mode is Linux-only; on
other platforms supply a separate
.I -data
directory.
.SH CONFLICTS
When a remote change arrives for a file that has also changed locally since the
last sync, the newer modification time wins the path and the other version is
kept. By default it goes to a
.B .drivel-conflicts
folder in the same directory, as
.IR "name (conflict YYYY-MM-DD HH-MM-SS).ext" ,
named for the losing version's modification time, and the folder syncs like any
other.
.B -conflicts
and
.B -conflicts-dir
choose another place: a folder kept on this machine or on the remote only, the
desktop trash, the backend's trash, or nowhere. A folder that is not set to sync
is left out in both directions, and moving a copy out of it restores the copy as
an ordinary file. A loser that cannot be kept is never overwritten: the operation
that would replace it fails and is retried. A conflict that an incoming change
and an outgoing upload meet at the same moment is decided once, by whichever
reaches it first. A copy is saved locally first and
uploaded in the background, and one that cannot be uploaded stays in the local
folder. Copies made before 18 October 2026 are
beside their file and stay local-only.
.PP
On a backend that reports no checksums, such as SFTP, a local change is
recognised by the size and modification time recorded at the last sync, or for a
file never synced by comparing its bytes with the remote's.
.PP
The same check runs before an upload. Before replacing a file it has synced
before,
.B drivel
asks whether the remote still holds what it last synced; if another machine has
changed it, that version is kept first and the local file is then uploaded. A
local file unchanged since the last sync is not uploaded over a newer remote at
all. A file with no sync history is checked too, as part of the upload itself on
Google Drive and SFTP, and anything already at its path is kept.
.B "-conflicts overwrite"
skips that check. Two uploads that start at practically the same moment can still
pass each other.
.PP
A deletion arriving from another machine removes a local file only if it still
holds what was last synced. A file edited here and not yet uploaded, or one never
synced, is kept and uploaded again; a deleted directory stays if anything in it
was kept.
.PP
The other direction is settled by the enumeration sweep. A file deleted here while
.B drivel
was not running, or whose deletion could not be sent, is deleted on the backend
when the backend still holds what was last synced \(em re-checked immediately
before the removal \(em and counts toward
.BR -max-deletes .
A remote copy edited since is kept and downloaded again, and a remote directory
is removed only once everything the sweep listed inside it has been.
.SH ABANDONED UPLOADS
Every push is retried with a growing delay between attempts \(em five attempts
over roughly seven seconds \(em and an error the backend reports as permanent is
not retried at all. Past that
.B drivel
gives up on the event and says so:
.PP
.RS
.EX
[sync] push write notes/todo.md failed after 5 attempt(s): \(rs
    ... (recorded for repair by the next sweep)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Giving up is deliberate: retrying a rejected upload forever spins, and a durable
queue of unsent work would be a second copy of the user's data to keep safe.
Instead the
.I path
is recorded, and the next enumeration is brought forward to a few minutes' time
so the file is re-sent from whatever is on disk by then.
.PP
.RS
.EX
[sweep] a push was abandoned: a repair sweep falls due in 5m0s
[sweep] repush  notes/todo.md (an earlier push was abandoned)
.EE
.RE
.PP
It is the
.I next
sweep rather than an immediate one, because a sweep is the most expensive
operation here and one transient failure does not warrant it; the delay also
folds a whole outage's worth of failures into a single sweep. Repeated failures
double the delay out to 80 minutes. And
.B -sweep-interval 0
does not switch any of this off.
.PP
An abandoned
.I deletion
is recorded too, and the same sweep re-makes it: with no local file to re-send,
it finds the remote object still matching what was last synced and the local
path gone, and deletes it on the backend under the same guards as any deletion a
sweep infers.
.SS Uploads lost to a crash
Recording the path only happens if
.B drivel
runs the code that records it, which a
.BR kill (1)
with
.BR SIGKILL ,
a power cut, an out-of-memory kill or a shutdown that ran out of time does not.
A sweep therefore has a second way to reach the same file: it records the size
and modification time of every file as it syncs it, and re-sends any local copy
that no longer matches. A mount marks its state database when it starts serving
and clears the mark only after it has drained at unmount, so a start that finds
the mark set \(em the previous run was killed, or its drain ran out of time \(em
always runs that sweep, whatever
.B -sweep-interval
says.
.PP
.RS
.EX
[sweep] repush  notes/todo.md \(rs
    (local copy diverged from its baseline; an earlier push never landed)
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same pass covers a file written into the backing directory while
.B drivel
was not running, though after a clean shutdown nothing forces the sweep that
finds it; use
.BR -resync .
It considers only files last modified
.I before
this mount started \(em anything newer is a change the running engine is already
answerable for, and a file held open and never closed is still sent on close as
described under
.BR -push-delay .
A baseline with no recorded size and time (anything last synced by a build
predating 10 September 2026) is counted in the sweep summary and left alone
rather than re-uploaded on a guess.
.SH FAILED DOWNLOADS
A remote change that cannot be applied locally \(em a download cut off partway,
a full disk, a name the backing filesystem refuses \(em does not stop inbound
sync: the change feed moves on, and the
.I path
is recorded and retried about a minute later.
.PP
.RS
.EX
[pull] a remote change could not be applied: retrying in 1m0s
[pull] refetch notes/todo.md (an earlier apply failed)
.EE
.RE
.PP
A retry asks the provider what the path holds
.I now
and applies that exactly as the feed would have, so a file changed again in the
meantime arrives at its newest version and a divergent local edit still gets its
conflict copy. No sweep is involved, so
.B -sweep-interval 0
does not switch it off. Repeated failures double the delay to about an hour; a
restart retries at once. A path that no longer exists remotely is dropped from
the list and nothing local is touched. A remote
.I deletion
that fails to apply is not retried: by then the local file may be new, unsent
work, and the next sweep's delete guards are the right place to decide.
.SH SPECIAL FILES AND MOUNT SAFETY
.B drivel
always mounts
.B nodev
and
.BR nosuid ,
and there is no option to disable them: a device node or a setuid binary arriving
from a remote is never something a user asked for. This protects the mountpoint
only \(em with
.I -data
the backing directory is an ordinary directory reachable without going through
.BR drivel ,
and nothing there is affected. On FreeBSD only
.B nosuid
is requested, because the kernel has no
.B nodev
flag and only
.B devfs
may hold device nodes.
.PP
.B Hard links are refused
with
.BR EPERM ,
the error
.BR link (2)
defines for a filesystem that cannot create them. A path\-addressed cloud store
cannot express two names sharing one copy of the bytes. Permitting the link would
leave two ordinary files that are both uploaded and diverge from each other on the
first write, having reported success.
.PP
.BR Symlinks ,
FIFOs, sockets and device nodes are created normally in the backing directory and
are never synced \(em they carry no byte stream, and no provider can represent
them.
.B drivel
logs one line naming each one, from the mount when it is created and from the
enumeration sweep when the local walk steps over it, so a file that is not syncing
says so rather than merely being absent elsewhere. On FreeBSD a FIFO or a device
node cannot be created through the mount at all: the attempt fails with
.B EINVAL
and creates nothing. Symlinks work normally there.
.PP
.B Permissions and the holes in a sparse file are not synced.
A file downloaded for the first time is created by the atomic write that protects
a reader from a half\-finished one, so it lands
.BR rw\-\-\-\-\-\-\- ;
a download that replaces a file keeps that file's permission bits, without
.BR setuid ,
.B setgid
or the sticky bit. An upload carries the file's modification time, and a
download is stamped with it, so a file is dated to its last edit everywhere
(whole seconds on SFTP); a block-wise SFTP update is dated to the upload. An
executable bit and a
.B setuid
bit reach no other client. A sparse file transfers
its full apparent size, and a download leaves its all\-zero 4 KiB blocks
unwritten, so it is sparse again on the receiving filesystem. The client that
wrote the file keeps its mode and holes unchanged. Carrying POSIX
mode and ownership needs a record bound to the content it describes and is not
implemented.
.SH PROVIDER BACKENDS
A storage backend runs in its own process, started by
.B drivel
and reached over a private socket.
.PP
The backends that ship with
.B drivel
\(em
.B gdrive
and
.B sftp
\(em are compiled into the binary, and
.B drivel
launches one by re-executing itself. Nothing has to be installed for them, there
is no search path involved, and the
.I kind
a config file names with
.BR provider " = " \(dq kind \(dq
is the one
.B drivel
asked for. A build made with the
.B nobundle
build tag carries none of them, and needs the executables below instead.
.PP
Any other backend is an executable named
.IB drivel-provider- kind
which
.B drivel
looks for in, in order: the directory holding the running
.B drivel
binary,
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/drivel/plugins ,
.IR /usr/local/lib/drivel/plugins ,
and
.IR /usr/lib/drivel/plugins .
The
.I kind
is taken from the filename and from nothing else. A backend compiled into the
binary wins over an installed file of the same kind, and the file it ignored is
named in the log; between two installed copies the first on the path wins, and
the other is named in the log the same way.
.PP
.B drivel
refuses to start a backend that is group- or world-writable, or one whose
directory is world-writable without the sticky bit: a backend runs with your
credentials, so a file someone else could replace between installation and launch
is their code holding them. The refusal names the file and the problem.
.PP
This is not a sandbox. A backend runs as the same user as
.BR drivel ,
with that user's filesystem access, and installing one is trusting it. What
.B drivel
does do is give it nothing it was not configured with: the environment is
rebuilt rather than inherited, carrying only
.BR PATH ,
.BR HOME ,
.BR TMPDIR ,
.BR TZ ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ *,
.BR SSL_CERT_FILE ,
.BR SSL_CERT_DIR ,
the proxy variables and
.BR SSH_AUTH_SOCK .
Ambient cloud credentials such as
.B GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS
are deliberately not passed on, and neither are the
.B XDG_
or
.B DRIVEL_
variables \(em so which account a mount uses is a fact about its configuration
rather than about the shell it was started from. The one
.B DRIVEL_
variable a backend does see is set by
.B drivel
itself rather than inherited: it names the descriptor the backend watches to
learn that
.B drivel
has gone.
.PP
A backend's lifetime is its mount's. Stopping a mount stops its backend, and so
does killing
.B drivel
outright \(em by
.BR "kill -9" ,
an out-of-memory kill or a crash \(em which is what that descriptor is for.
Before this, a backend survived a killed
.B drivel
and went on holding that account's credentials with nothing driving it. If you
ever find one, it is named
.B plugin-serve
in its command line.
.SH ENVIRONMENT AND REQUIREMENTS
.B drivel
requires the FUSE mount helper
.RB ( fusermount3 ),
which ships with the system
.B fuse3
package (install with
.B "dnf install fuse3"
or
.BR "apt install fuse3" ).
HTTP/3/QUIC benefits from a larger UDP receive buffer; if
.B quic-go
warns about the buffer size, raise it with
.BR "sysctl -w net.core.rmem_max=7500000" .
.TP
.B DRIVEL_PLUGIN_PATH
A colon-separated list of directories to search for provider backends. It
.I replaces
the default list rather than extending it. Not inherited by a backend.
.SH PLATFORM SUPPORT
.B drivel
is built and tested on
.BR Linux ,
on every architecture Go supports, and on
.B FreeBSD
15.1, where the full test suite has been run with nothing skipped. It also
compiles for
.B macOS
(amd64 and arm64), but that build is
.I unverified
\(em it has never been run against a live Drive. Treat it accordingly.
.B Windows
is not supported and is not planned; use WSL2, or Google's native Drive client.
Other Unixes (OpenBSD, NetBSD, Solaris, illumos, AIX) lack a Go FUSE binding.
.PP
Five things differ off Linux; the third and fourth hold on every platform, and
are here because it is where they bite.
.TP
.B In-place mode is unavailable
It routes backing I/O through
.RI ( /proc/self/fd/N ),
and only Linux has procfs. Supply
.I -data
on other platforms.
.TP
.B -lazy is untested on macOS
Lazy hydration marks undownloaded files with an extended attribute, which is what
prevents an empty placeholder being uploaded over live remote content.
.B drivel
implements extended attributes on all three platforms, and FreeBSD has been
verified, but the code has never been run on a Mac: treat lazy mode there as
untested and prefer the default eager mode.
.TP
.B -lazy is unsafe without extended attributes, on any platform
If the marker cannot be written there is no record that a file is a placeholder at
all, and an unfetched file is indistinguishable from an empty one. This covers a
.I -data
directory on exFAT or FAT, and on a FreeBSD
.I tmpfs
(UFS and ZFS carry extended attributes;
.I tmpfs
does not). Windows drives under WSL2 depend on the Windows filesystem rather than
on WSL: over NTFS the attributes are carried and the marker survives a
.BR "wsl --shutdown" ,
over exFAT they are not. Prefer the Linux filesystem inside WSL regardless, since
NTFS is case-insensitive and two names differing only in case collide into one
local path.
.B drivel
refuses to start a lazy mount there, naming the directory: the fix is eager mode
or a different
.IR -data ,
not preserving any database.
.TP
.B A case-insensitive backing store holds one of two names
macOS's default volumes, NTFS and a Linux
.B casefold
directory treat names differing only in case (on APFS, also in Unicode
normalisation) as one file, where the backend need not.
.B drivel
warns at startup when the backing store folds names, keeps the file already
there when the backend holds two such names, and skips the other with a line
naming both, rather than writing one over the other.
.TP
.B macOS backing directories belong on APFS or HFS+
On exFAT, FAT and some network volumes macOS keeps extended attributes in hidden
.I ._name
companion files rather than in the filesystem, which would place the placeholder
marker inside the synced tree.
.B drivel
detects this and reports the store as having no extended attributes.
.PP
On macOS,
.B macFUSE
is the only supported FUSE implementation:
.B drivel
looks for
.I mount_macfuse
or
.I mount_osxfuse
and nothing else, so
.B FUSE-T
does not work. macFUSE 4.x is not open source and its own terms restrict commercial
use;
.B drivel
neither bundles nor distributes it, and its MPL\-2.0 licence is unaffected by your
use of it \(em the two are separate programs communicating over a file descriptor,
not a combined work.
.SH SIGNALS
.B drivel mount
unmounts cleanly on
.B SIGINT
.RB ( Ctrl-C )
or
.BR SIGTERM :
the mount is unmounted first (flushing pending FUSE events), then the sync engine
drains its bounded queue of in-flight uploads before the process exits. A second
.B Ctrl-C
forces an immediate exit.
.B drivel stop
(with
.BR -control )
takes the same path, and the log says which of the two it was.
.PP
The drain is bounded even when the backend has stopped answering: 30 seconds
after the request, anything still waiting for an upload slot is recorded for
repair instead, and the next start sweeps for it. An unmount that fails because
the mountpoint is busy is retried every second until it succeeds.
.PP
A backend process started by
.B drivel
ignores
.BR SIGTERM :
it is ended by
.B drivel
itself, or when
.B drivel
exits, so a signal sent to every process at once \(em as
.BR systemd (1)
does at shutdown \(em cannot stop it while uploads are still draining through
it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/drivel/control.sock
The control socket, while a mount started with
.B -control
is running. Removed when it exits.
.TP
.I /sbin/mount.fuse.drivel
Symlink to the drivel binary, making it the
.BR mount (8)
helper for the
.B fuse.drivel
filesystem type.
.I /sbin/mount.drivel
is the same thing for the
.B drivel
type. Both are placed by
.BR "make install" .
.TP
.I $PREFIX/lib/drivel/plugins/drivel-provider-*
Provider backends as separate executables, placed by
.B "make install"
only for a build that carries none of its own, where
.I $PREFIX
defaults to
.IR /usr/local .
See
.BR "PROVIDER BACKENDS" .
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/drivel/plugins/
Provider backends installed for one user. Defaults to
.I ~/.local/share/drivel/plugins
when
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME
is unset.
.TP
.I /etc/fstab
Mounts brought up by
.B mount
and
.BR "mount -a" ; see
.BR "MOUNT HELPER" .
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/drivel/config.toml
Mount and account configuration; see
.BR CONFIGURATION .
Read when
.B drivel mount
is given neither
.B -config
nor
.BR -mount .
Defaults to
.I ~/.config/drivel/config.toml
when
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/drivel/<account>/
Per-account credentials and token, created mode 0700 by
.BR "drivel login -account" .
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/drivel/<name>/state.db
Default sync-state database for a configured mount, one per mount. Defaults under
.I ~/.local/state
when
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME
is unset.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/drivel/mounts/<account>/
Backing store written into the config file by
.BR "drivel login -account" ,
one per mount. Defaults under
.I ~/.local/share
when
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME
is unset. This holds your files rather than a copy of them, which is why it is
here and not under
.IR $XDG_CACHE_HOME :
that directory advertises itself as safe to delete at any time.
.TP
.I credentials.json
OAuth client secret (client ID/secret). Treat as a secret; do not commit.
.TP
.I token.json
Cached OAuth token from
.BR "drivel login" .
.TP
.I drivel-state.db
bbolt sync-state database (change-feed cursor + echo records). Deleting it loses
the record of what has already been synced, which is recoverable but not free: see
.BR "RECOVERING FROM A DAMAGED DATABASE" .
.TP
.I drivel-state.db.corrupt
The previous
.I drivel-state.db
when
.B drivel
found it unreadable and replaced it. Kept for inspection; only the most recent one
is kept, and nothing reads it.
.TP
.I drivel-index.db
bbolt path\(->ID index for the provider. A cache; safe to delete.
.TP
.I drivel-index.db.corrupt
The previous
.I drivel-index.db
when
.B drivel
found it unreadable and replaced it. Kept for inspection; safe to delete.
.SH RECOVERING FROM A DAMAGED DATABASE
.PP
If
.I drivel-state.db
cannot be read \(em a power cut mid-write, a failing disk \(em
.B drivel
renames it with a
.B .corrupt
suffix, starts with an empty one, and logs that it did so. It does not refuse to
mount, because an unattended mount from
.I /etc/fstab
has nobody to intervene, and because an empty database is a state
.B drivel
already handles: it is the same position as a first-ever mount.
.PP
No file is deleted as a result. A deletion is only ever inferred from a record
that the path was previously synced, so with no records
.B drivel
reads every file it finds as new rather than as deleted. Lazily hydrated files are
also unaffected, because what marks a file as a placeholder is stored on the file
itself and not in this database.
.PP
Two things follow, and both are visible:
.IP \(bu 2
The remote tree is enumerated in full before the change feed is tailed again,
which costs what a first sync costs.
.IP \(bu 2
Any path whose local and remote copies differ gets a conflict copy, because with
no history there is nothing to say which side changed \(em see
.I data-safety.md
for what a conflict copy is named and where it lands.
.PP
The same happens to
.IR drivel-index.db ,
with no consequences at all beyond some extra requests while it warms up again:
every entry in it is re-checked against the provider before it is used, so a cold
index costs lookups rather than correctness.
.PP
A database that fails to open for any other reason is unchanged \(em fatal for the
sync state, and for the index a warning followed by running without it. A database
already locked by another
.B drivel
process is never replaced.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Authenticate:
.PP
.RS
.EX
drivel login
.EE
.RE
.PP
Mount with a separate backing directory (log-only, no cloud):
.PP
.RS
.EX
drivel mount -mount ./mnt -data ./data
.EE
.RE
.PP
Mount in-place on Linux (the directory is its own backing store):
.PP
.RS
.EX
drivel mount -mount ./dir
.EE
.RE
.PP
Mount with Google Drive sync:
.PP
.RS
.EX
drivel mount -mount ./mnt -data ./data \e
    -credentials credentials.json -token token.json -drive-root <folderID>
.EE
.RE
.PP
Set up two accounts and serve both from one process:
.PP
.RS
.EX
drivel login \-account personal
drivel login \-account work
$EDITOR ~/.config/drivel/config.toml   # add a [[mount]] for each
drivel mount
.EE
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B drivel
exits
.B 0
on a clean unmount,
.B 2
on an unknown command or flag-parse error, and non-zero on a fatal mount, auth,
or sync-setup error.
